:name: custom_watch tier-1 sim
:description: Boots the custom_watch firmware ELF on an emulated nRF52840 DK — no hardware needed. Normally started via bin/watch-sim.sh, which builds the ELF, decodes the defmt stream, and feeds the canned NMEA fixture into the GPS UART. The ELF is the exact thumbv7em build that watch-flash.sh flashes; nothing is compiled differently for the simulator.

using sysbus

$elf ?= @apps/custom_watch/target/thumbv7em-none-eabihf/release/app
$defmt_out ?= @/tmp/watch-sim/defmt.raw
$gps_pty ?= @/tmp/watch-sim/gps-pty
$phone_port ?= 7788

path add $ORIGIN

mach create "watch"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/cpus/nrf52840.repl

# RTC1 with a working OVRFLW event + all four compare channels. The stock
# platform breaks the firmware's 64-bit monotonic clock two ways: rtc1 is
# declared with numberOfEvents: 3 (the real RTC1 has 4 — embassy-nrf's time
# driver parks its half-period tick on CC[3], which a 3-channel model silently
# drops) and the model's OVRFLW event is an unimplemented tag. Either alone
# stalls the period counter, so Instant::now() wrapped to zero every 512 s
# (2^24 ticks @ 32768 Hz) and froze every uptime-anchored path (fix
# acceptance, moving time, alerts) 8.5 min into a run. The replacement model
# (sim/NRF52840_RTC_Overflow.cs) is the upstream v1.16.1 source plus a real
# OVRFLW; re-registering with numberOfEvents: 4 restores CC[3].
include @NRF52840_RTC_Overflow.cs
machine UnregisterFromParent sysbus.rtc1
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
rtc1: Timers.NRF52840_RTC_Overflow @ sysbus 0x40011000
    numberOfEvents: 4
    -> nvic@17
"""

# MAX86177 optical-HR AFE on the TWISPI0 I2C bus (0x40003000, address 0x62 —
# the bus + address boards/nrf52840_dk's HrPort assigns and the hr task
# probes). Two models: the stock platform's twi0 is the legacy-TWI-only
# NRF52840_I2C, which implements none of the TWIM EasyDMA registers
# embassy-nrf's Twim driver programs (ENABLE=6, TXD/RXD PTR+MAXCNT, the
# LASTTX/LASTRX shorts, EVENTS_STOPPED) — the hr task's presence probe timed
# out against it and the whole optical-HR path was bench-gated.
# NRF52840_TWIM.cs is the TWIM (EasyDMA) master; Max86177.cs synthesizes a
# deterministic tagged MEAS1(PPG)/MEAS2(ambient) sample stream at 100 Hz off
# the virtual clock (~72 BPM by default). Scenario knobs, settable live from
# the monitor (documented in Max86177.cs):
#   sysbus.twi0.max86177 AmbientLevel 200000    # common-mode ambient bleed
#   sysbus.twi0.max86177 PulseAmplitude 0       # 0 = no pulse (off-wrist)
#   sysbus.twi0.max86177 DcBaseline 500         # reflected DC at drive 0x40
#   sysbus.twi0.max86177 PulsePeriodSamples 83  # BPM = 6000 / period
include @NRF52840_TWIM.cs
include @Max86177.cs
# BMP581 barometer on the TWIM1 bus (the baro task probes TWISPI1 at 0x46).
# Two pieces in one block: the stock platform's twi1 is the legacy nRF51-style
# TWI byte-interface model, which maps none of the TWIM EasyDMA registers
# embassy-nrf's twim driver programs (ENABLE=6, TXD/RXD.PTR+MAXCNT, the
# LASTTX/LASTRX shortcut matrix) — every transaction hung, so the baro task's
# timeout-probe parked it as "no BMP581". NRF52840_TWIM.cs is a TWIM master
# model that completes transfers synchronously; BMP581.cs is the sensor behind
# it with a deterministic, monitor-scriptable altitude source (see its header:
# SetAltitudeMeters / StartTriangleProfile / SetSeaLevelPa via
# `sysbus.twi1.bmp581 ...` in the monitor). IRQ 4 per the datasheet (the
# shared SPIM1/TWIM1 slot).
include @BMP581.cs
machine UnregisterFromParent sysbus.twi0
machine UnregisterFromParent sysbus.twi1
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
twi0: I2C.NRF52840_TWIM @ sysbus 0x40003000
    -> nvic@3

max86177: Sensors.MAX86177 @ twi0 0x62

twi1: I2C.NRF52840_TWIM @ sysbus 0x40004000
    -> nvic@4

bmp581: Sensors.BMP581 @ twi1 0x46
"""

# Board LEDs. We declare these inline instead of loading
# platforms/boards/nrf52840dk_nrf52840.repl because that repl also declares the
# four user buttons as Miscellaneous.Button peripherals that drive their pins
# LOW at reset (active-low, non-inverted) — which the firmware's pulled-up
# button inputs would read as "pressed". The buttons are injected by driving the
# GPIO input pins directly (the btnN macros below), so no Button peripheral is
# needed; leaving the pins to the firmware's internal pull-up keeps them idle
# high (released) until a macro pulls one low. Only led0 needs a name (the
# liveness hook), but all four are kept to mirror boards/nrf52840_dk.
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
gpio0:
    13 -> led0@0
    14 -> led1@0
    15 -> led2@0
    16 -> led3@0

led0: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpio0 13
    invert: true

led1: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpio0 14
    invert: true

led2: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpio0 15
    invert: true

led3: Miscellaneous.LED @ gpio0 16
    invert: true
"""

# Sharp Memory LCD on SPIM3, chip-select on P1.12 (matches the pin map in
# boards/nrf52840_dk). The model is runtime-compiled C#; `showAnalyzer
# sysbus.spi3.display` (watch-sim.sh --gui) opens the live panel window.
# The model draws a BTN1..BTN4 bezel under the LCD and implements
# IAbsolutePositionPointerInput, so the analyzer window auto-attaches it as
# the machine's pointer: clicking a bezel button drives the same gpio0 pin
# the btnN macros below pull (buttonPort). Mouse down = press, up = release.
include @SharpMipDisplay.cs
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
// SPIM3: the stock platform declares spi0..2 without EasyDMA and repl
// overlays can't change a constructor argument, but the 0x4002F000 slot
// (the dedicated high-speed SPIM the firmware uses for the display) is
// free to declare fresh with EasyDMA on. IRQ 47 per the datasheet.
spi3: SPI.NRF52840_SPI @ sysbus 0x4002F000
    easyDMA: true
    -> nvic@47

display: Video.SharpMipDisplay @ spi3
    buttonPort: gpio0

gpio1:
    12 -> display@0
"""

macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadELF $elf
"""
runMacro $reset

include @defmt_rtt.py
setup_defmt_rtt $defmt_out

# Button injection. The firmware's button task (built with the `sim-buttons`
# feature by bin/watch-sim.sh) polls the button pins' levels — it can't use a
# hardware edge because embassy's wait_for_falling_edge rides the nRF GPIO
# SENSE/DETECT path, which this Renode GPIO model doesn't implement. So `click`
# drives the input pin directly: pull it LOW (a press, active-low) for a short
# window — long enough for the ~10 ms poll to catch the falling edge — then
# drive it back HIGH (release). Driving the pin (OnGPIO) is what moves the IN
# register the firmware reads; a Miscellaneous.Button's Press does not propagate
# on these pins. Invoke from the monitor with `runMacro $btn3` (a bare `btn3`
# does not run a macro). One call = one clean press.
python
"""
import time

def click(pin, hold=0.3):
    gpio = monitor.Machine["sysbus.gpio0"]
    gpio.OnGPIO(pin, False)  # press: pull the active-low input down
    time.sleep(hold)
    gpio.OnGPIO(pin, True)   # release: back to idle high
"""

# BTN1 (P0.11) — start / pause / resume the recording.
macro btn1
"""
    python "click(11)"
"""
# BTN2 (P0.12) — stop the recording.
macro btn2
"""
    python "click(12)"
"""
# BTN3 (P0.24) — cycle the run-view page (dashboard / distance / pace / lap).
macro btn3
"""
    python "click(24)"
"""
# BTN4 (P0.25) — take a manual lap.
macro btn4
"""
    python "click(25)"
"""

# LED1 (P0.13) blinks at 1 Hz from ui::blink_task — echo each state change
# into the Renode log as a scheduler-liveness signal.
python
"""
led = monitor.Machine["sysbus.gpio0.led0"]

def led_changed(led, state):
    led.InfoLog("LED1 %s" % ("on" if state else "off"))

led.StateChanged += led_changed
"""

# Phone-link rail: the firmware's status frames leave on uart1 (UARTE1);
# a TCP server socket exposes them to the host. The mobile app's dev
# screen connects here (Android emulator: host 10.0.2.2).
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $phone_port "phone" false
connector Connect sysbus.uart1 phone

# GPS rail: the u-blox breakout's NMEA stream arrives on uart0 (UARTE0).
# The pty end is where the host feeder writes canned sentences. The firmware
# consumes them once step 3 of README.md (GNSS bring-up) lands; until then
# the emulated UARTE discards them. Kept as the LAST setup command: monitor
# errors don't reach the Renode log file, so bin/watch-sim.sh treats the pty
# symlink appearing as proof the whole script executed.
emulation CreateUartPtyTerminal "gps" $gps_pty true
connector Connect sysbus.uart0 gps

start
